  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A strong upper trough is diving southwards across Quebec \nProvince, but the attendant surface low is far northeast of our \narea. A stretched out frontal boundary will sag southwards while a \ntongue of Pacific moisture rounds an upper ridge axis across the \ncentral US as we approach dawn. Southwest to west flow against the \nfrontal boundary will produce intervals of showers, one along the \ninternational border this morning, and then more widespread activity \nthis afternoon and evening. Convection this afternoon will likely be \nshort. There's about 500 J/kg of CAPE, but the equilibrium levels \nwill barely make it above -20 C. It will be tough to generate much \ncloud ice. So thunderstorms should be fairly scarce.\n\nPrecipitation will decline with sunset as the front pushes south and \ndaytime heating ends. High pressure will begin to nose in with \nsomewhat cooler air behind the front. A seasonable night of mid 40s \nto mid 50s is expected with dry, pleasant conditions much of Monday. \nTowards the end of Monday, precipitation will approach from the \nsouthwest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Upper troughing will continue to dominate far eastern \nCanada, and this northwest flow will continue to prevent a surge of \nwarm air from reaching this area. A sluggish warm front will slowly \nshift northeast while mid-level moisture moves northwest to \nsoutheast over the hump of a 500mb ridge axis extending into James \nBay. So we'll observe precipitation grazing the region Monday night, \nwith the highest rain chances across southern St. Lawrence County, \nand then these rain chances slowly eke northeast Tuesday morning \ninto the afternoon. There remains some uncertainty with the \nplacement of precipitation. The mid-level moisture could be the \nremnants of convection in the Great Lakes region, and where exactly \nthe quasi-stationary front is located will play a large role in \ndeciding the focus of heavier rain. Guidance is slightly farther \nnortheast this evening, but not by much. Most atmospheric \ninstability will be aloft Tuesday. Near surface conditions will be \nmostly stable, and so any storm activity will be garden variety.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: During the middle and end of the week, an upper level \ntrough will gradually shunt a ridge over the Midwest and Mid-\nAtlantic to the south, preventing any heat from building into the \nregion. The last of a couple ridge runner type features will move \nthrough Wednesday night into Thursday, likely bringing an area of \nheavy rain with convective enhancement from elevated instability. \nThe warm sector will not be fully established over the area so \nlimited surface instability should limit the threat for severe \nweather. However, increasing PWATs and good dynamics will cause an \naxis of heavy rain to develop, though its exact placement is still \nuncertain. The flood threat looks low at this point but will \ncontinue to be watched. For any flash flooding to occur, the band on \nWednesday night into Thursday would likely have to occur in the \nexact location as the heavier bands in the beginning of the week for \na chance. NBM probabilities of 2+ inches of rain are up to around 20 \npercent for southern Vermont, the part of the region that has the \nhighest probability of seeing the heaviest rain. GEFS 25th-75th \npercentiles range from a quarter to three quarters of an inch at \nthis time, which does neglect localized higher amounts due to \nconvection. However, trends still continue to prog the best moisture \nand forcing to our southwest. Brief high pressure will nudge in form \nthe northwest late Thursday into Friday with seasonable conditions. \nTrends have begun to take more of a wet turn from previous forecasts \nfor next weekend, with our Wed/Thu system occluding off the Canadian \nMaritimes, keeping cyclonic flow over and shower chances over our \narea.",
